id=a52sxq_devicecare_sleep_lists
name=Device Care: list every restrictable app in the sleeping app lists
author=kusti420
description=Battery > Background usage limits > Sleeping / Deep sleeping / Never auto sleeping apps > Add. (1) The Add lists use canRestrictWithReason instead of canRestrict and ignore only its widget (0x8) and quick panel tile (0x100000) bits (canRestrict also applied the server deep sleep exception list and adjust-restriction 21). (2) MARs only manages apps that have a row in SDHMS' ForcedAppStandby table and SDHMS never gives one to an app on the doze allowlist (Battery "Unrestricted"), so such apps were in none of the lists and restrict() on them did nothing. User-installed apps on the user doze allowlist are now offered under Sleeping and Deep sleeping apps (not Never auto sleeping: they are already exempt). Adding one takes it off the allowlist (= Battery "Optimized"), inserts its FAS row the way SDHMS does on install, waits up to 4 s until MARs manages it and, for deep sleep, re-checks MARs' own protections (device admin, default apps, keyboard, wallpaper, system gallery role, protected packages) before the stock code restricts it; on a veto the allowlist entry is restored. Apps that re-allowlist themselves (Samsung platform-signed Good Lock modules with DEVICE_POWER) leave the list again when they next start. (3) v3: apps that own a contact-syncing account (WhatsApp, Telegram, Signal...) can be deep slept by the user. MARs' ImportantRoleFilter (filter 22) blocked them because deep sleep (DISABLED_UNTIL_USED by "auto_disabler") made RegisteredServicesCache drop the app's authenticator and sync adapter, AccountManager then hid the account and the contacts provider deleted its contacts. framework.jar RegisteredServicesCache (system_server only) now also matches components of MARs-deep-sleeping packages (Samsung flag 0x20000000, as Samsung already does for activities), so account, contacts and sync settings survive and the app is still not woken; services.jar ImportantRoleFilter keeps the system gallery role check and lets K4ContactSleep drop the contact half for the manual check and for packages the user put to deep sleep (MARs' own automatic deep sleep still skips them).
